{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>And Adam lived a hundred and thirty years and begot in his own likeness, after his image.</b> This verse testifies that Cain — Adam did not beget him, and he was not in his likeness after his image. And his separation from his wife was 130 years: from the day he was created there was a reason, for such a span of time was needed until there were born from him and from her those fit to be born, and these are spirits (רוחין) and demons (שדין) and liliths (לילין). And as they expounded: all those 130 years that Adam separated from his wife, female spirits would warm themselves from Adam and give birth to males, and male spirits would warm themselves from the woman and give birth to females. And so we find with Noah, that he did not beget until 500 years, and there was a reason: had he raised offspring at the age of 70 or 100 years, his seed and his seed's seed would have been consumed before the Flood came; therefore the Holy One, blessed be He, restrained his urge and he did not beget until 500 years. For Japheth his eldest son was punished before the Flood came and was not worthy to be saved by that great miracle, and the same judgment applies to the rest of the generations who lived long days, that in each and every one there is a reason, if only we knew and grasped it." "<b>And know</b> that Scripture divided the lives of the ancients in those generations into three parts. The first: it mentioned how many years Adam had before he begot. The second: after he begot Peleg. The third: that it summed them all. But the reason corresponds to the lifespan of a person, which is divided into three parts: childhood, youth, and old age." "<b>And you should consider</b> that in all the generations from Adam to Noah, Scripture mentioned concerning each one ״and he died,״ and it was not necessary, for since death was decreed upon Adam the first man, it is certain that all his offspring died, for the branches perish when the root perishes. But the matter was to mention concerning each one ״and he died,״ because there were among them some righteous men, such as Methuselah and those like him, and had Scripture not informed us and mentioned concerning each one ״and he died,״ a person might have thought that they were swept away by the decree of the Flood and did not die upon their beds as does all mankind. Therefore it treated them all with one practice, mentioning concerning each one ״and he died,״ whereas in the genealogy of Noah, which begins from Noah onward, it did not mention ״and he died״ at all, for the Flood had already passed and it is known that they died their own death."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 5, :notes {0 "The text reads ול היה זוכה, apparently a typo for ולא היה זוכה; translated as 'and was not worthy.'"}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:10:34.137808667Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}